[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m [response] To determine the number of vertical asymptotes of the function \( y = \frac{2}{x^2 + x - 6} \), we need to identify the values of \( x \) that make the denominator zero, as these are the points where the function is undefined and potentially has vertical asymptotes.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m First, we factor the quadratic expression in the denominator:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m \[ x^2 + x - 6 = (x + 3)(x - 2). \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m So, the function can be rewritten as:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m \[ y = \frac{2}{(x + 3)(x - 2)}. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m The function will have vertical asymptotes at the values of \( x \) that make the denominator zero, provided these values do not also make the numerator zero. In this case, the numerator is a constant (2), which is never zero. Therefore, the vertical asymptotes occur at the roots of the denominator:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m \[ x + 3 = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad x - 2 = 0. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m Solving these equations, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m \[ x = -3 \quad \text{or} \quad x = 2. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
