[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m [response] To determine how many of the first 500 positive integers are divisible by 3, 4, and 5, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of these three numbers. The LCM of 3, 4, and 5 is calculated as follows:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m - The prime factorization of 3 is \(3\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m - The prime factorization of 4 is \(2^2\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m - The prime factorization of 5 is \(5\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m The LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m \[ \text{LCM}(3, 4, 5) = 2^2 \times 3 \times 5 = 4 \times 3 \times 5 = 60. \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m So, we need to find how many multiples of 60 are there among the first 500 positive integers. This is equivalent to finding the largest integer \(k\) such that \(60k \leq 500\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
