[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m First, consider the range of \(\sin x\) for \(0^\circ < x < 180^\circ\). The sine function takes values in the interval \((0, 1)\) in this range. Therefore, \(\sqrt{\sin x}\) takes values in the interval \((0, 1)\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m Next, we need to determine the range of \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\sin x}) \). Since \(\sqrt{\sin x}\) ranges from \(0\) to \(1\) (excluding \(0\) and \(1\)), we need to find the corresponding range of the logarithm. Specifically, we need to find the range of \(\log_2 t\) for \(0 < t < 1\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m The function \(\log_2 t\) is a decreasing function for \(0 < t < 1\). As \(t\) approaches \(0\) from the right, \(\log_2 t\) approaches \(-\infty\). As \(t\) approaches \(1\) from the left, \(\log_2 t\) approaches \(0\). Therefore, the range of \(\log_2 (\sqrt{\sin x})\) is \((-\infty, 0)\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=42128)[0m Thus, the range of the function \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\sin x}) \) for \( 0^\circ < x < 180^\circ \) is \(\boxed{(-\infty, 0)}\).
